course: biology
lesson: 137
title: Kung Fu Panda: real mantises, snakes and monkeys
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Furious Five are named after real kung fu styles, and those styles were named after real animals that people watched closely: tiger, crane, mantis, snake and monkey.
explain: A real praying mantis waits as still as a twig, then its spiky front legs snap shut on a fly faster than you can blink; it can even turn its head and look you in the eye.
explain: A snake has no arms or legs, yet it climbs trees and follows a mouse's trail by flicking its forked tongue to pick up smells.
explain: Monkey swings by his tail, but here the film slips: no monkey from Asia or Africa can hang by its tail; only some monkeys of Central and South America can.
fact: Praying mantises wait very still, then shoot out spiky front legs that fold like a pocket knife to grab insects in a fraction of a second; a mantis has six legs and walks on the back four.
fact: There is a real Chinese martial art called Praying Mantis style, said to have been inspired by watching a mantis fight.
fact: Mantises can turn their heads about 180 degrees to follow prey without moving their bodies, and scientists put tiny 3D glasses on them to show they judge distance with two eyes.
fact: Many mantises are camouflaged as leaves, sticks, bark or flowers, and the orchid mantis looks so like a pink and white flower that insects fly to it.
fact: Many mantises have a single ear in the middle of their chest that hears hunting bats, so they dive or spiral away, though with one ear they cannot tell which side the bat is on.
fact: Snakes have no legs or arms but hundreds of vertebrae and ribs; they move by pushing scales and muscles against the ground, and some climb trees by gripping bark with belly scales.
fact: Snakes smell with their tongues: the tongue carries bits of smell to a special organ in the roof of the mouth, and its forked tips tell left from right.
fact: Snakes have no outside ears but feel vibrations through their jaw bones; they have no eyelids, so they stare without blinking, but they cannot hypnotise anyone.
fact: Pit vipers have small pits on their faces that sense heat, so they can find warm mice and birds in the dark; China has many kinds of snakes, and most try to avoid people.
fact: No monkeys from Asia or Africa can hang by their tails; only some monkeys from Central and South America have gripping tails, and spider monkeys have bare, ridged skin under the tail for grip.
fact: Golden snub-nosed monkeys live in snowy mountain forests in China, have thick fur, huddle in groups to keep warm, and their tiny upturned noses may help prevent frostbite.
fact: Macaques are clever, bold monkeys of Asia, including China, that learn from each other and take easy food from people; some Japanese macaques bathe in hot springs in winter.
fact: Kung fu styles named after tiger, crane, mantis, snake and monkey copy each animal's power, balance, speed, bending or grip.
exercise: How does a praying mantis catch insects? | It waits still, then grabs them with spiky front legs | It chases them through the air for hours until they are tired | It spits sticky glue at them | It stings them with its tail
exercise: How many legs does a mantis have? | Six | Four | Eight | Two
exercise: What is Praying Mantis style? | A real Chinese martial art said to copy a mantis | A kind of green mantis that lives only in the hills of China | A mantis dance at weddings | A name made up for the film
exercise: About how far can a mantis turn its head? | About 180 degrees | Not at all, its head is fixed in place | About 10 degrees each way | All the way round twice
exercise: How did scientists show mantises judge distance with two eyes? | They put tiny 3D glasses on them | They covered them in paint | They taught them to read the numbers on a ruler | They blew bubbles at them
exercise: What does an orchid mantis look like? | A pink and white flower | A thin green stick on a bush | A dry brown leaf on the ground | A smooth black stone in a stream
exercise: Where is a mantis's single ear? | In the middle of its chest | On the very top of its head | On its front legs | On its wing tips
exercise: What does a mantis do when it hears a hunting bat? | Dives or spirals away | Flies straight at the bat | Freezes in the air and hangs still | Sings back at it to scare it off
exercise: How does a snake move with no legs? | By pushing its scales and muscles against the ground | By rolling along like a wheel down the hill, very fast | By hopping on its tail | By swimming through the air on its flat ribs
exercise: Why does a snake flick out its forked tongue? | To pick up smells and tell which side they are stronger | To taste the air for little drops of water when it is thirsty | To scare enemies away | To catch flies like a frog
exercise: How does a snake sense sounds with no outside ears? | It feels vibrations through its jaw bones | It hears through its tongue when it flicks it out | It listens with its tail | It cannot sense sound at all
exercise: What do the pits on a pit viper's face sense? | Heat from warm animals | Light from the Moon | Sounds from far away | The taste of water
exercise: Which monkeys can hang by their tails? | Only some monkeys of Central and South America | All monkeys in the world, from every continent | Only monkeys from China | Only monkeys from Africa
exercise: What helps a spider monkey's tail grip? | Bare ridged skin underneath, like a fingerprint | Tiny sharp claws on the very tip of the tail, like a cat's | Sticky glue from its skin | Hooks made of bone
exercise: How do golden snub-nosed monkeys survive the snowy winters? | Thick fur and huddling together | Sleeping in hot springs every night | Flying south to warmer places for the winter | Digging deep burrows under the snow
exercise: Why do macaques take food from tourists? | They are clever and learn easy food from each other | They are kept hungry on purpose by the park guards all year | They think tourists are trees | They only eat crisps
exercise: Which animals are real kung fu styles named after? | Tiger, crane, mantis, snake and monkey | Shark, dolphin, eagle, ant and bee | Panda, cow, pig, sheep and goat | Lion, zebra, hippo, rhino and giraffe
test: «7 mantises in the jar lol how many legs is that» | 42 | 28 | 56 | 14
test: «5 mantises, how many ears in total» | 5 | 10 | 0 | 20
test: «Monkey from China swings by his tail, all monkeys do» What is right? | No monkey from Asia or Africa can hang by its tail; only some American ones can | Yes, every monkey can hang by its tail, wherever in the whole world it lives | Yes, Chinese monkeys have the strongest tails | No monkey anywhere can grip with its tail
test: In The Jungle Book, Kaa the python hypnotises Mowgli with spinning eyes. What is real? | Snakes cannot hypnotise anyone | All pythons hypnotise their prey | Snakes can hypnotise only children | Kaa's eyes really glow in the dark
test: «snake's licking the air, is it thirsty» What is it really doing? | Picking up smells with its tongue | Drinking little drops of rain from the air | Trying to bite the flies in the air | Cooling its mouth down on a hot day
test: «green stick on the bush just moved, wtf» What is it most likely? | A mantis or stick insect camouflaged as a stick | A snake pretending to be a stick so that birds leave it alone | A twig moved by magic | A leaf eating a fly
test: «snakes are deaf coz they got no ears» What is right? | They have no outside ears but feel vibrations through their jaws | Yes, snakes sense nothing at all | They hear through their scales, which work like the skin of a drum | They hear through holes in their tails
test: What would happen if a mantis lost its chest ear? | It could not hear bats coming and would be easier to catch | It could not see its prey any more and would slowly starve to death | It could not walk anymore | Nothing at all would change
test: «monkeys in the park nicked my sandwich, cheeky sods» What is true about macaques? | They are clever and learn from each other where easy food is | They never touch human food | They are trained by the keepers to steal food from the visitors | They only steal shiny things
test: In a pitch-dark forest, how can a pit viper find a sleeping mouse? | The pits on its face sense the mouse's body heat | It sees in the dark with glowing eyes | It hears the mouse's heartbeat with its big ears | It waits for morning
test: «why do golden monkeys have tiny squashed noses, so ugly» What may the reason be? | A tiny upturned nose may help prevent frostbite | They broke them in fights with other monkeys long ago | It helps them smell bananas | It is just bad luck
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the capacity of monkeys to suspend themselves by the tail. | This capacity is confined to certain monkeys of Central and South America | All monkeys possess this capacity | This capacity is characteristic of the monkeys of China, the golden monkey above all | This capacity is confined to the monkeys of Africa
exam: Identify the function served by the forked tongue of the snake. | It conveys particles of scent to an organ in the roof of the mouth and indicates their direction | It serves to drink water from the air | It is employed principally for the intimidation of enemies, which retreat upon seeing it | It serves to capture flying insects in the manner of a frog
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hearing of the praying mantis? | Many mantises possess a single ear in the chest by which they detect the calls of bats | The mantis possesses two ears upon its head, situated just behind the large compound eyes | The mantis hears by means of its antennae alone | The mantis is entirely without the sense of hearing
exam: Ascertain the faculty by which the pit viper detects warm-blooded prey in darkness. | Pits upon the face which are sensitive to heat | Eyes which emit light of their own | External ears of exceptional sensitivity | The sense of taste in its scales
